__pycache__/
*.pyc
*.egg-info/
.venv/
build/
dist/
# generated data (regenerate from configs / re-drop per docs/LOCAL_DATA_SETUP.md)
data/cache/
data/three_gene_dataset/*.h5
data/datasets/*/payload.h5
data/staging/

# RUN RECORDS ARE TRACKED. `experiments/` used to be ignored wholesale, which meant the
# audit trail behind every number in docs/ was absent from version control: you could read
# a claim but not the run that produced it. This project is headed for a paper, so the
# evidence is versioned alongside the claims. It is affordable because the model is tiny
# (3N^2+3N parameters): a whole run directory is ~15 KB and the run index ~5 KB per 30 runs.
#
# RECOVERED MODELS ARE TRACKED TOO. A checkpoint is ~3 KB here (the model has 3N^2+3N
# parameters), and it is what `rngrn evaluate --run-id ...` reloads to re-simulate a
# recovered network. results/train_results.json carries the same parameters as text for
# reading and citing; the .pt carries them in the form you can RUN. Both, deliberately:
# text survives a torch upgrade, the checkpoint reproduces the dynamics exactly.
#
#   tracked  — runs.jsonl / target_reports.jsonl (the append-only indexes),
#              config/frozen_config.yaml (the config that ACTUALLY ran),
#              results/*.json (metrics, provenance, recovered parameters + Jacobian),
#              checkpoints/*.pt (the runnable recovered model),
#              exp*.json / exp*.csv (the experiment-script evidence ledger)
#   ignored  — only regenerable bulk: figures, and any raw field dump. A rollout field at
#              96x96xN float64 is ~220 KB per run and is fully reproducible from the
#              checkpoint plus the frozen config, so it is not worth versioning.
experiments/**/figures/
experiments/**/*.h5
experiments/**/*.npz
experiments/**/*.npy
experiments/**/*.png

# downloaded literature (not ours to redistribute)
papers/
